  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a strong downtrend (e.g., Example 1) but the model forecasts a steady uptick, apply a negative bias correction of -5% to -8% immediately, as true outcomes often reverse sharply downward despite bullish sentiment.",
    "If the model forecast is based on a flat or slightly rising trend while recent history contains high volatility spikes (e.g., Example 3/4), reduce the forecasted trajectory by 10-15% to account for mean reversion and lack of sustained momentum.",
    "When sentiment history shows mixed signals (positive and negative values) with no dominant trend, assume the model's linear projection is invalid; cap forecasts at the highest recent historical price or apply a -20% correction to prevent overshooting volatile ranges.",
    "In scenarios where the model predicts a gradual increase over 30 days but true outcomes show immediate volatility followed by a decline (e.g., Example 5), shift the forecast curve downward by 10-12% and flatten the slope to reflect higher uncertainty.",
    "If sentiment history is predominantly neutral or slightly positive while recent prices are at multi-week lows, apply a -15% correction to the model's upward bias, as market participants often ignore minor sentiment shifts during deep value corrections."
  ]
}
